<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 18: Introduction to 4-momentum</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> Reading covers sections 7.1 through 7.5. 
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Let's use the procedure at the very end of page 191 to find the Newtonian momentum
            <m>m\vec{v}</m> of a particle, 
            using the diagram in the figure. Explain in which sense two observers at rest with respect to one 
            another agree that the momentum they obtain using the events A and B and their time interval 
            <m>\Delta t</m> is <q>the same vector</q>, even though they clearly measure different components 
            for it.                
        </p>

        <image width="50%">
            <shortdescription>Components of a vector using two coordinate systems</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                Two coordinate systems xy and x<prime/>y<prime/> that share a common origin, point A,are shown. 
                The x y coordinate system has the x axis pointing to the right and the y axis pointing upwards. 
                The primed coordinate system has perpendicular axes rotated counterclockwise with respect to the x y coordinates.
              </p>
              <p>
                Another point in the plane, labeled B, is shown. The vector <m>\Delta \vector{x}</m> that connects A and B
                is also labeled.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                     \draw[very thick,-&gt;,&gt;=stealth] (0,0)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
                     \draw[very thick,-&gt;,&gt;=stealth] (0,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};

                     \draw[very thick,-&gt;,&gt;=stealth] (0,0)--(20:3) node[above] {$x'$};
                     \draw[very thick,-&gt;,&gt;=stealth] (0,0)--(110:3) node[right] {$y'$};
                     \draw[fill=black] (0,0) circle (0.05) node[below] {$A$};

                     \draw[fill=black] (50:3) circle (0.05) node[above] {$B$};
                     \draw[color={rgb,255:red,221; green,34; blue,119},-&gt;,&gt;=stealth,ultra thick] (0,0)--(50:3) node[midway,left] {$\Delta \vec{x}$};

                     \draw[dashed,thick] (50:3)--++(-1.92836,0);
                     \draw[dashed,thick] (50:3)--++(0,-2.298133);

                     \draw[dotted,thick] (50:3)--++(200:2.5980762);
                     \draw[dotted,thick] (50:3)--++(290:1.5);
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Let's say we insist on using <m>\vec{p}=m\vec{v}</m> but recognize that momentum should be part of some 
            4-D object. We will therefore use the definition (7-1) to define a 4-D momentum but use the coordinate 
            time instead of the proper time. Calculate the magnitude of this 4-D vector described in section 4.3 
            and show that different observers measure different lengths for this arrow. Using your discussion of 
            question 1, does this seem like an appropriate definition of momentum?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		The picture below shows an elastic collision which conserves the Newtonian momentum
                <m>m\vec{v}</m>
        </p>

        <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz18/before-after.svg" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>A before and after picture of an elastic collision</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                Top frame: Before a collision, a particle of mass <m>2m</m> is moving at speed <m>c/5</m> to the right.
                A particle of mass <m>m</m>, to its right, is at rest.
              </p>
              <p>
                Bottom frame: after the collision, the particle of mass <m>2m</m> moves at speed
                <m>c/15</m> to the right. The particle of mass <m>m</m> moves at speed
                <m>4c/15</m> to the right.
              </p>
            </description>
        
        </image>
        <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the velocity addition formula to draw this before-and-after picture in the reference frame
                    where the particle of mass <m>2m</m> blob is at rest <alert>after the collision</alert>. If you
                    find numbers instead of exact fractions, keep many significant figures.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Use your new before-and-after picture to show that the Newtonian momentum <m>m\vec{v}</m> is not conserved
                    in the new reference frame. This is a general fact: if the Newtonian momentum remains constant
                    in one reference frame, it will <alert>not remain constant in every frame.</alert> Thus, 
                    conservation of momentum using the old definition cannot be a law of physics.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>